{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"verymad-hard-weaverbird","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# compact-on-close holds the store write lock for MINUTES, starving concurrent writers — and \"isolated\" worktrees share one store\n\n## The two facts together are the defect\n\n### 1. The worktrees are NOT isolated — they symlink to ONE store\n\n`/Users/joeoakhart/agent-5200-diag/.tickets-tracker` is a **symlink** to\n`/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.tickets-tracker`. So is `/Users/joeoakhart/probe-status-e34a/.tickets-tracker`.\nAll three resolve to the same path and to the **same lock inode (420945058)**.\n`lock.canonical_tracker` (`_store/lock.py:124-127`) realpaths deliberately so that they contend on one\nlock — which is correct behaviour for correctness, and means every parallel agent session serialises\nthrough a single write lock even though each believes it has its own worktree.\n\nTwo live `rebar transition ... closed` processes were running against this store during the incident\n(from `agent-5200-diag` and `probe-status-e34a`).\n\n### 2. compact-on-close holds that lock across an entire reduce + retire + git commit\n\n`_commands/compact.py:178` takes the same write lock and holds it across the whole critical section\n(`compact.py:186` onward), and it is invoked right after every close at\n`_commands/transition_close.py:92-104`.\n\nMeasured from the tracker's own git log: a **2m17s gap** with nothing landing between\n`2a8f02ce14 SIGNATURE e39f` (12:28:38) and `b2e28d2273 COMPACT e39f` (12:30:55). The next commit is\nd582's STATUS at 12:30:56 — one second after the compaction released.\n\n### The consequence\n\nA concurrent writer's 60s lock budget (`lock.py:73-74`: 30s x 2 attempts) is consumed entirely inside\none compaction window. That is what dropped a durable record in\n[rebar:ab54-f19f-d5bd-422c] — the close's PASS `COMPLETION_VERDICT` sidecar got its single 60s shot\nduring the gap and lost it.\n\n`flock` is also not FIFO, so a waiter can additionally be starved by a stream of short writers rather\nthan by one long holder.\n\nCorroborating symptom: `2bc7-d77b-641c-42aa` carries **two** PASS sidecars (12:34:22 and 12:36:31) —\na close that had to be retried under the same contention.\n\n### Diagnosability gap found along the way\n\n**The lock records no holder identity that survives contention.** The fcntl leg (`lock.py:552`) records\nno pid at all, and the only owner stamp lives in `.ticket-write.lock.d/owner` (`lock.py:36`), which is\nremoved on release — so a timed-out waiter cannot say who blocked it. The holder above was identified by\ninferring from the commit timeline, not from any recorded pid. A `LockTimeout` that cannot name the\nholder is expensive to diagnose under fleet load.\n\nAlso observed, separately: `llm/enrich_drain.py:243-251`'s \"cheap gate\" logs\n`enrich drain gate check took 300-800ms (> 20 ms budget)` on nearly every command against this\n3174-ticket store, and `overlap_drain=async` detaches drain CHILD processes that write events\n(`enrich_drain.py:254-256`), making them additional lock contenders. None committed inside the measured\ngap, so they are not the holder here.\n\n### Residue\n\nAn untracked `.tickets-tracker/.tmp-event-lpusw0uh` (mtime 10:42) predates this incident and belongs to\nsome other failed or killed write. `event_append.py:470-472` does unlink the staging temp on\n`LockTimeout`, so this is from a different failure mode — worth sweeping and worth knowing that\nsomething leaves temps behind.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] compaction no longer holds the store write lock across its whole reduce + git-commit section —\n      either it takes the lock only for the final swap, or it yields between phases, measured by the\n      commit-gap shrinking under a concurrent-writer test\n- [ ] a `LockTimeout` names the holder: the lock records an owner (pid + worktree) that a timed-out\n      waiter can read and report, so diagnosis does not require timeline inference\n- [ ] concurrent close from two worktrees sharing one store does not drop a durable record — asserted\n      by a test that closes from two processes against one tracker\n- [ ] the shared-store-by-symlink topology is documented where agents are told to use a fresh worktree,\n      since \"isolated worktree\" currently implies an isolated store and does not get one\n- [ ] the stray `.tmp-event-*` residue is swept, and its source failure mode is identified or explicitly\n      recorded as unknown\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit -k \"lock or compact or concurren\" -q\n    make lint && make typecheck\n\nA concurrent-writer reproduction is the load-bearing test: two processes closing tickets against one\ncanonical tracker, asserting both closes land every durable record. Without it this is a timing bug that\nonly reappears under fleet load.","id":"7084-4e33-e698-42dd","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"compact-on-close holds the store write lock for minutes, starving concurrent writers; worktrees share one store by symlink"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785872504314031001,"uuid":"b08e0344-c65a-4dc3-8998-91592ea32f4b"}